  "before_interfaces": {
    "def-aqft-local-operator-and-dimension": [
      null,
      "## Definition\n\nUse natural units $c=\\hbar=1$ on four-dimensional Minkowski space $M=\\mathbb R^{1,3}$ with coordinates $x^0=ct,x^1,x^2,x^3$ and metric $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$. Fix a complex affine algebraic group $G$ with no nontrivial closed normal unipotent subgroup, where unipotent means that every nonzero finite-dimensional rational module has a nonzero fixed vector; this is the reductive hypothesis. Fix a finite set $I$ of field species. For each $i\\in I$, fix a finite-dimensional complex $\\mathbb Z/2$-graded vector space $\\Phi_i=\\Phi_i^{\\bar 0}\\oplus\\Phi_i^{\\bar 1}$, a field parity, and an algebraic representation $\\rho_i:G\\to\\operatorname{GL}(\\Phi_i)$. Include the declared spacetime-index action in $G$ or in its specified finite-dimensional representation data. A field configuration assigns smooth component functions on $M$ to the even generators and formal odd Grassmann generators with smooth coefficient functions to the odd generators.\n\nFix a derivative cutoff $r\\geq0$, a maximum polynomial field degree $q\\geq0$, and a rational engineering weight $\\Delta_i$ for each field species. A derivative $\\partial_\\mu$ has engineering weight one, and an $s$-jet generator is a component of $\\partial_{\\mu_1}\\cdots\\partial_{\\mu_s}\\phi_i(x)$ for $0\\leq s\\leq r$. If covariant derivatives are used, their finite jet generators and $G$-action are included explicitly in the same data. Let $J_r$ be the resulting finite-dimensional $\\mathbb Z/2$-graded vector space with its specified algebraic representation $\\rho_r:G\\to\\operatorname{GL}(J_r)$. Its polynomial algebra is the supercommutative algebra $P_r=\\operatorname{Sym}(J_{r,\\bar 0}^{*})\\otimes\\Lambda(J_{r,\\bar 1}^{*})$: even generators commute and odd generators anticommute. For a fixed weight $D\\in\\mathbb Q$, let $P_{r,q,D}$ be the complex span of monomials of total engineering weight $D$ and total polynomial degree at most $q$.\n\nA local operator in this finite-order data is an element of $P_{r,q,D}$ evaluated at $x$ on the $r$-jet of the fields. A scalar operator invariant under the declared symmetries is an element of $V_{r,q,D}=P_{r,q,D}^{G}$. Engineering dimension means this assigned mass weight; it is not a statement about an observed quantity. In four dimensions a term with operator $\\mathcal O_D$ has the convention $C\\mathcal O_D/\\Lambda^{D-4}$, where $\\Lambda$ has mass dimension one and $C$ is dimensionless. Reality conditions, when required for a real action, are additional conjugation data on $V_{r,q,D}$."
    ],
    "lem-aqft-invariant-quotient-exactness": [
      null,
      "## Statement\n\nAssume the Axiom of Choice. Let $G$ be a complex reductive affine algebraic group, let $V$ be a finite-dimensional complex vector space with an algebraic representation $\\rho:G\\to\\operatorname{GL}(V)$, and let $N\\leq V$ be a linear subspace satisfying $\\rho(g)N=N$ for every $g\\in G$. Define $V^G=\\{v\\in V:\\rho(g)v=v\\text{ for all }g\\in G\\}$, give $V/N$ the induced representation, and let $\\pi:V\\to V/N$ be the quotient map. Then the restriction $\\pi^G:V^G\\to(V/N)^G$ is surjective and has kernel $V^G\\cap N$. Consequently the map\n\n$$\nV^G/(V^G\\cap N)\\longrightarrow(V/N)^G,\\qquad v+(V^G\\cap N)\\longmapsto v+N\n$$\n\nis a canonical complex-linear isomorphism."
    ],
    "lem-aqft-finite-order-local-operator-space-finite-dimensional": [
      null,
      "## Statement\n\nLet $J=J_{\\bar 0}\\oplus J_{\\bar 1}$ be a finite-dimensional $\\mathbb Z/2$-graded complex vector space with a finite homogeneous basis $z_1,\\ldots,z_n$, each generator assigned a rational engineering weight. Form $P=\\operatorname{Sym}(J_{\\bar 0}^{*})\\otimes\\Lambda(J_{\\bar 1}^{*})$. For finite $q\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\geq0}$ and any $D\\in\\mathbb Q$, let $P_{q,D}$ be the span of supercommutative monomials of total generator degree at most $q$ and total engineering weight $D$. Then $P_{q,D}$ is finite-dimensional. If a group acts linearly on $J$ preserving parity and the weight grading, its invariant subspace $P_{q,D}^{G}$ is also finite-dimensional."
    ],
    "def-aqft-lsz-amplitude-data": [
      null,
      "## Definition\n\nFix the field data and Minkowski convention from the earlier finite-jet definition and a truncation order $N\\geq0$. A regulated perturbative generating datum consists, for each regulator label $\\rho$, of a finite-dimensional $\\mathbb Z/2$-graded complex space $X_\\rho$ of field-mode coordinates, a specified integration functional $d\\mu_\\rho$ on its even/odd coordinates, a local action $S_{\\rho,\\epsilon}:X_\\rho\\to\\mathbb C[\\epsilon]/(\\epsilon^{N+1})$, and a generating functional\n\n$$\nZ_{\\rho,\\epsilon}[J]=\\int_{X_\\rho}\\exp\\!\\bigl(iS_{\\rho,\\epsilon}(\\varphi)+i\\langle J,\\varphi\\rangle\\bigr)\\,d\\mu_\\rho(\\varphi),\\qquad Z_{\\rho,\\epsilon}[0]\\text{ is a unit},\n$$\n\nwhere $J\\in X_\\rho^*$ is a linear source and the integral is either an actual convergent finite-dimensional integral or a specified formal perturbative integral. “A unit” means that the constant coefficient of $Z_{\\rho,\\epsilon}[0]$ is nonzero, so the normalized functional $Z_{\\rho,\\epsilon}[J]/Z_{\\rho,\\epsilon}[0]$ is defined in the truncated ring. An admissible field-coordinate change is a local, parity-preserving formal map $F_{\\rho,\\epsilon}:X_\\rho\\to X_\\rho$ with $F_{\\rho,0}=\\operatorname{id}$ and an inverse modulo $\\epsilon^{N+1}$; local means each output field at $x$ depends on a finite jet at $x$. Its regulated Jacobian factor $\\mathcal J_{F,\\rho}$ is the factor specified by the exact change-of-variables identity\n\n$$\n\\int h(\\varphi)\\,d\\mu_\\rho(\\varphi)=\\int h(F_{\\rho,\\epsilon}(\\psi))\\,\\mathcal J_{F,\\rho}(\\psi)\\,d\\mu_\\rho(\\psi)\n$$\n\nfor every admissible integrand $h$; no assertion that this factor is $1$ is part of the definition. In the transformed coordinates the source is $J\\cdot F_{\\rho,\\epsilon}(\\psi)$, so the field insertion remains the same named original coordinate.\n\nAn LSZ amplitude datum is an additional limit of connected momentum-space Green functions as regulators are removed, when that limit exists. The connected $n$-point function is $i^{-n}$ times the $n$th source derivative of $\\log(Z_{\\rho,\\epsilon}[J]/Z_{\\rho,\\epsilon}[0])$ at $J=0$, Fourier transformed with convention $\\widetilde f(p)=\\int e^{ip\\cdot x}f(x)\\,d^4x$; the overall momentum-conservation delta function is removed. For each external one-particle channel $a$ (with a fixed polarization component when the field has spin), the datum specifies a mass $m_a>0$ and nonzero simple-pole residue $Z_a$ at $s_a=m_a^2$, where $s_a=-\\eta^{\\mu\\nu}p_{a,\\mu}p_{a,\\nu}$. Here “stable external channel” means precisely that this isolated pole exists with nonzero residue and zero width. The transformed interpolating fields must have an invertible one-particle overlap matrix; this is part of the datum. For every $n$-point function it requires pole factorization\n\n$$\nG_n(p_1,\\ldots,p_n)=\\left(\\prod_{j=1}^n\\frac{Z_{a_j}^{1/2}}{s_j-m_{a_j}^2}\\right)\\mathcal A_n(p_1,\\ldots,p_n)+\\text{terms missing at least one external pole}.\n$$\n\nThe normalized LSZ amplitude is the joint residue\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A_n=\\left(\\prod_{j=1}^n Z_{a_j}^{-1/2}\\right)\\lim_{s_j\\to m_{a_j}^2}\\left(\\prod_{j=1}^n(s_j-m_{a_j}^2)\\right)G_n.\n$$\n\nThis definition supplies only the regulator and pole data needed for a conditional amplitude statement. It does not assert that a continuum limit, stable particles, or LSZ poles exist for a given quantum field theory."
    ],
    "def-aqft-operator-basis-quotient": [
      null,
      "## Definition\n\nFix a finite jet order $r$, maximum polynomial field degree $q$, engineering weight $D$, finite field-species list, coefficient field $\\mathbb C$, and complex reductive affine algebraic group $G$ with the finite-dimensional rational action specified on the jet generators, including the declared spacetime-index symmetries. Let $W_D$ be the finite-dimensional space of all local polynomials of weight $D$ and degree at most $q$, before imposing $G$-invariance, and let $V_D=W_D^G$ be its invariant scalar subspace. An admitted raw relation space is a specified $G$-stable linear subspace $\\widetilde R_D\\leq W_D$, given by explicit linear identities among the chosen polynomials. Its invariant part is $R_D=\\widetilde R_D\\cap V_D$. Any action, boundary, or observable interpretation of the selected identities is extra mathematical data and must be stated with the result that verifies it.\n\nThe finite-order invariant operator-basis quotient is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal Q_D:=V_D/R_D.\n$$\n\nTwo invariant operators represent the same class exactly when their difference lies in $R_D$. An operator basis is any finite list of representatives in $V_D$ of a vector-space basis of $\\mathcal Q_D$. The quotient records precisely the declared relation space; it does not assert that every relation relevant to an arbitrary EFT, observable, or infinite operator tower has been included."
    ],
    "prop-aqft-integration-by-parts-eom-and-field-redefinition-redundancy": [
      null,
      "## Statement\n\nAssume full AC. Use four-dimensional natural units $c=\\hbar=1$ and a finite jet-polynomial field model with its declared finite rational action of a complex reductive affine algebraic group $G$. Let $W_D$ be a finite raw local-polynomial space at fixed engineering weight $D$, derivative cutoff, and field-degree cutoff; let $V_D=W_D^G$. Let $\\widetilde R_D$ be the $G$-stable linear span in $W_D$ of the declared polynomial identities, admitted total-divergence densities, and leading-equation densities $F^a\\mathcal E_a^{(0)}$ defined below, with $R_D=\\widetilde R_D\\cap V_D$. The finite-order invariant operator quotient is $\\mathcal Q_D=V_D/R_D$.\n\nLet $M=\\mathbb R^{1,3}$ with $x^0=ct$, $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, and volume measure $d^4x$. Even field components are smooth maps $M\\to\\Phi_a^{\\bar0}$ and odd components are smooth coefficient families in a fixed Grassmann algebra valued in $\\Phi_a^{\\bar1}$; admitted variations and currents have compact support. Let $\\mathcal I[L]=\\int_M L\\,d^4x$ on admitted local densities, with the boundary convention $\\mathcal I(\\partial_\\mu J^\\mu)=0$. Let $S_0[\\phi]$ and each $S_j[\\phi]$ be $G$-invariant local polynomial functionals of these field configurations, so their formal Taylor expansions through order $N$ are defined. Let the variational derivative $\\mathcal E_a^{(0)}[\\phi]$ of $S_0$ be defined by\n\n$$\nD S_0[\\phi](h)=\\mathcal I\\!\\left(\\sum_a h^a\\mathcal E_a^{(0)}[\\phi]\\right)\n$$\n\nfor every admitted compactly supported parity-compatible variation $h$. A leading-equation relation is a density $F^a\\mathcal E_a^{(0)}$ with local, compact-support-preserving, parity-compatible $G$-equivariant $F^a[\\phi]$ for which this density lies in $W_D$; an integration-by-parts relation is a density $\\partial_\\mu J^\\mu$ satisfying the stated boundary convention. Consider a finite formal action $S_\\epsilon=S_0+\\sum_{j=1}^N\\epsilon^jS_j$ in the ring $\\mathbb C[\\epsilon]/(\\epsilon^{N+1})$, where $\\epsilon$ is dimensionless and each $\\epsilon^kF^a$ has the units of $\\phi^a$. For every $1\\leq k\\leq N$, the local identity-leading change of variables $\\Psi_k(\\phi)=\\phi-\\epsilon^kF[\\phi]$ is invertible as a formal map through order $N$ and satisfies\n\n$$\nS_\\epsilon[\\Psi_k(\\phi)]=S_\\epsilon[\\phi]-\\epsilon^k\\mathcal I\\!\\left(F^a\\mathcal E_a^{(0)}[\\phi]\\right)+O(\\epsilon^{k+1}).\n$$\n\nThus a coefficient $+\\epsilon^k\\mathcal I(F^a\\mathcal E_a^{(0)})$ can be removed at order $k$ without changing lower-order coefficients, provided all induced terms through the retained order are kept. The quotient $\\mathcal Q_D$ identifies exactly the declared polynomial, boundary, and leading-equation relation span; it asserts no equivalence of arbitrary off-shell Green functions and does not by itself assert a quantum measure or amplitude equivalence."
    ],
    "thm-aqft-field-redefinition-observable-equivalence-at-fixed-order": [
      null,
      "## Statement\n\nAssume full AC. Let $S_\\epsilon$ be a local $G$-invariant action through order $N$, with coefficients in $\\mathbb C[\\epsilon]/(\\epsilon^{N+1})$, where $G$ is the declared complex reductive symmetry group. Let $F_\\epsilon=\\operatorname{id}+O(\\epsilon^k)$ be a parity-preserving local formal coordinate map with a local inverse modulo $\\epsilon^{N+1}$, for some $1\\leq k\\leq N$, and suppose its order-$k$ difference is an admitted relation in the finite-order operator-basis quotient. For each regulator $\\rho$, assume a regulated generating functional whose zero-source partition value is a unit in $\\mathbb C[\\epsilon]/(\\epsilon^{N+1})$ and an exact change-of-variables density factor $\\mathcal J_{F,\\rho}$ satisfying\n\n$$\n\\int h(\\varphi)\\,d\\mu_\\rho(\\varphi)=\\int h(F_{\\rho,\\epsilon}(\\psi))\\,\\mathcal J_{F,\\rho}(\\psi)\\,d\\mu_\\rho(\\psi)\n$$\n\nfor every admissible integrand $h$. Define the transformed data using action $S_\\epsilon\\circ F_\\epsilon$, this full Jacobian factor, and source $J\\cdot F_\\epsilon(\\psi)$. Assume the common regulator limit has stable external channels $a_j$ with masses $m_{a_j}>0$, simple poles with nonzero residues $Z_{a_j}$ at $s_j=m_{a_j}^2$, where $s_j=-\\eta^{\\mu\\nu}p_{j,\\mu}p_{j,\\nu}$, and connected functions that factor as\n\n$$\nG_n(p_1,\\ldots,p_n)=\\left(\\prod_{j=1}^n\\frac{Z_{a_j}^{1/2}}{s_j-m_{a_j}^2}\\right)\\mathcal A_n(p_1,\\ldots,p_n)+\\text{terms missing at least one external pole}.\n$$\n\nHere $s_j=-\\eta^{\\mu\\nu}p_{j,\\mu}p_{j,\\nu}$ with $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$. The normalized amplitude is the joint residue $\\mathcal A_n=(\\prod_j Z_{a_j}^{-1/2})\\lim_{s_j\\to m_{a_j}^2}(\\prod_j(s_j-m_{a_j}^2))G_n$. Then the normalized source generating functionals agree through order $N$, and every normalized on-shell LSZ amplitude agrees coefficientwise modulo $\\epsilon^{N+1}$. The action-level relation is a change of field coordinates with induced higher-order terms; it is not equality of the two off-shell action expressions. The conclusion does not apply after dropping the Jacobian, leaving the source untransformed, discarding induced terms, or asserting nonexistent continuum or unstable-particle LSZ limits."
    ],
